WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.600
Welcome to The Explainer. We're tackling a topic today that, honestly, is secretly the backbone of the entire software industry.

2
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:16.040
Now, you might be thinking to yourself, software versioning? Isn't that just kind of a bureaucratic chore for developers? Absolutely not. No way.

3
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.380
Today, we're going to reframe exactly how you look at those little numbers separated by dots.

4
00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:27.940
Versioning is a critical communication tool. It's basically the invisible glue that binds product managers, developers, and customers all together.

5
00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:30.660
It signals stability, value, and intent.

6
00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:36.180
So let's unpack exactly how you can turn what just seems like boring math into a rock-solid foundation of trust.

7
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.180
Okay, let's dive into this.

8
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:43.680
Before we look at the right way to do things, let's just paint a picture of the absolute chaos of an unversioned world.

9
00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:49.460
Imagine a reality where every single time you deploy code, you are basically spinning a roulette wheel.

10
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:56.540
A customer reports a bug, but because there's no clear version identifier, your support team has absolutely zero way to reproduce it.

11
00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.060
What exact state of the software are they even using?

12
00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.760
Without versions, you're literally flying blind.

13
00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.320
And the consequences of this chaos?

14
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:05.660
They're severe.

15
00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:10.440
Just imagine it's Monday morning and your team pushes a seemingly tiny update,

16
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.360
maybe just changing a data type or renaming a parameter.

17
00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:19.280
Suddenly, boom, 500 enterprise clients who built their internal systems on top of your public API

18
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.880
come crashing down all at once.

19
00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.940
Because you didn't manage or communicate that version change properly,

20
00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.980
their integrations are instantly broken.

21
00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:28.660
And worse, when you inevitably try to roll back

22
00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:30.560
to a stable state, you can't.

23
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.700
You have no fixed anchor points in your history.

24
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.300
It's pretty much a guaranteed way

25
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.040
to completely destroy customer trust overnight.

26
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.600
So here is the journey we're taking today.

27
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.660
First, the versioning contract.

28
00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:44.060
Two, semantic versioning rules.

29
00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.940
Three, comparing Semver and Calver.

30
00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.560
Four, API versioning and sunsets.

31
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.700
Five, smart release deployments.

32
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:53.500
And six, automation.

33
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:54.940
Let's get right to it.

34
00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:58.900
Section 1. The Versioning Contract. More than just math.

35
00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:04.120
You see, a version number is this vital tool that signals stability to everyone involved,

36
00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:09.220
especially your non-technical stakeholders. For product managers, it dictates roadmaps and guides

37
00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:13.780
marketing communications. I mean, it explains why a version 2.0 is a massive launch event,

38
00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:19.200
while a version 1.4.3 is just a quiet maintenance update. It even impacts service-level agreements.

39
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.420
I want you to remember this core metaphor. A version is a binding contract between the creator

40
00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:28.640
and the consumer. When you publish a version number, you are making a promise. You're telling

41
00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.280
them exactly what they can expect from this update. You're saying, if you see this number

42
00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.120
change in this specific way, here is exactly what will happen to your code. And that predictability,

43
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.140
that's exactly how long-term product adoption and customer satisfaction are secured.

44
00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:46.040
Section 2. Semantic Versioning Rules Explained. The Industry Standard.

45
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:52.640
Now, what's really interesting about this slide is how brilliantly simple yet strict this system is.

46
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.660
Created back in 2011 by GitHub co-founder Tom Preston-Werner,

47
00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:59.540
Semver is the universal language of software compatibility.

48
00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:03.360
It is the gold standard used by major ecosystems, like NPM,

49
00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:05.860
to communicate exactly how an update is going to behave.

50
00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:10.400
It uses this three-part format, major.minor.patch.

51
00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.620
You bump the patch number when you release a backward-compatible bug fix.

52
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.060
It's completely safe for the user.

53
00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.380
You bump the minor number when you add a new feature,

54
00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:19.380
but it still remains fully backward-compatible.

55
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.200
Again, totally safe to upgrade.

56
00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.820
But you bump the major number when you introduce a breaking change.

57
00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.520
This yells to the user, stop, you are going to need to adapt your code.

58
00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:30.420
And crucially, there's a reset rule.

59
00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.860
When you bump a higher component, everything to its right resets to zero.

60
00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:39.980
So version 1.4.2, getting a new feature, becomes 1.5.0, not 1.5.2.

61
00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:45.920
Actually, if you take away only one thing from this entire explainer, let it be this golden rule right here.

62
00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.080
Any breaking change absolutely requires a major version bump.

63
00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:57.740
If you remove a function, if you change a parameter name, or, you know, if you make an optional field mandatory, you are breaking the contract.

64
00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:05.100
The central question a developer literally must ask before a release is, will an existing user have to change their code to keep things working?

65
00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.220
If the answer is yes, it's a major update. Period.

66
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:14.360
Section 3. Comparing Semver and Calver. Choosing the right model for your users.

67
00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:22.340
And this brilliantly illustrates that while semantic versioning is incredible, it isn't some magic silver bullet for every single situation.

68
00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:24.440
Even its creator admits that.

69
00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:29.640
Choosing the right versioning model depends entirely on who is actually consuming your product.

70
00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:33.060
When you compare them side by side, the contrast makes perfect sense.

71
00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:39.900
Semver is absolutely mandatory when you're building libraries, packages, or APIs where other developers' code depends on yours.

72
00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:41.220
It signals compatibility.

73
00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:46.800
But look over at Calver, which uses the release date as the version number, like year.month.

74
00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:51.380
Calver is perfect for end-user applications, operating systems, or SaaS products

75
00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:54.060
where users aren't programmatically integrating with your code.

76
00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.800
Like when a user sees Ubuntu 22.04, they instantly know the software is from April 2022.

77
00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:01.380
It signals age.

78
00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:06.440
That allows PMs and users to immediately understand how outdated a current version might be.

79
00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:11.080
Section 4. API Versioning and Sunsets. Protecting your integration.

80
00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:15.160
All right, let's step into the high-stakes world of public APIs.

81
00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:21.320
As we talked about earlier, breaking a client integration is pretty much the ultimate cardinal sin in software development.

82
00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:27.340
An API requires intense versioning discipline to ensure stability and keep your customers' trust intact.

83
00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:30.500
Now, there are three main strategies for routing API versions.

84
00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:34.640
First up, you have the URL path, like slash v2 slash users.

85
00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:39.540
This is by far the most universally adopted method because it's incredibly visible and transparent,

86
00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.940
even if it does slightly clutter up the URL.

87
00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:43.920
Second is the query parameter,

88
00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.460
where you just add question mark version equals two

89
00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:47.380
to the end of the URL.

90
00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:48.460
It's super simple,

91
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.740
but honestly, developers can easily overlook it.

92
00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:52.600
Third is using a custom header.

93
00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.720
This keeps the URL beautifully clean,

94
00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:56.800
but it makes manual testing directly in a browser

95
00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:57.740
a whole lot harder.

96
00:05:58.260 --> 00:05:59.180
Whichever path you choose,

97
00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:00.820
consistency is the name of the game.

98
00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.420
To minimize the knee for those major API version bumps

99
00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:05.080
in the first place,

100
00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:06.900
you really should adopt Postal's law.

101
00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:09.680
You might also hear it called the robustness principle.

102
00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:14.240
Basically, when designing an API, be liberal in what you accept from the client.

103
00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:20.020
If a client sends an unrecognized field in their request, your API should safely ignore it rather than just crashing.

104
00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:25.620
Building robust APIs that survive non-breaking changes, that is a hallmark of truly excellent engineering.

105
00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:29.240
But look, eventually you will need to make a breaking change.

106
00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.380
You've got to move from version 1 to version 2.

107
00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:34.780
So how do you do that without completely infuriating your users?

108
00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.800
You execute a graceful API sunset.

109
00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:42.240
Step one, release v2, but keep v1 running simultaneously.

110
00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.660
Step two, mark v1 as depreciated.

111
00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:48.180
It still works, but you add a sunset header

112
00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:49.420
signaling that it's going away.

113
00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:52.640
Step three, communicate clearly with your clients,

114
00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:55.560
giving them a really obvious dedicated migration path.

115
00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:59.860
And finally, step four, only after a generous grace period.

116
00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.280
We're talking usually six to 12 months here.

117
00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:04.360
Do you actually shut down v1?

118
00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:08.840
This is the ultimate test of customer trust, giving them plenty of time to adapt.

119
00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:14.520
Section 5. Smart Release and Deployment, Ensuring Stable Transitions

120
00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:19.660
So, you've managed your version numbers and greasefully handled your API changes.

121
00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:24.740
But how do PMs and developers actually balance a really aggressive roadmap of new releases

122
00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:26.780
with that core promise of stability?

123
00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:30.320
Well, you don't just chuck the code onto the servers and cross your fingers.

124
00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:35.120
You use smart deployment strategies to safely enforce your versioning contract out in the wild.

125
00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:39.740
In a Canary release, you roll out the new version to just 1-5% of your users.

126
00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:43.500
If it crashes, you've only impacted a tiny fraction of your base.

127
00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:47.300
A blue-green deployment uses two perfectly identical environments.

128
00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:51.060
You deploy the new version to the idle environment, switch the traffic over,

129
00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:54.920
and if something goes sideways, you have an instant single-switch rollback to the old version.

130
00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:55.940
No panic needed.

131
00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:59.720
And a rolling deployment gradually shifts the update server by server,

132
00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:02.460
ensuring absolutely zero downtime for the user.

133
00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:06.440
Section 6. Automation via Conventional Commits

134
00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.520
Letting Machines Do the Paperwork

135
00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:13.480
Let's move to the really fun stuff and see how this builds into a fully automated machine.

136
00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:17.320
Managing version numbers manually is practically begging for human error.

137
00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:21.580
People forget to bump a patch or they jump to a major version just for marketing reasons

138
00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:23.460
without actually introducing breaking changes.

139
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:24.800
The solution here?

140
00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:28.460
Letting machines do the paperwork through a standard called conventional commits.

141
00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.700
Developers simply add a standard prefix to their commit messages.

142
00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:36.220
If they write fix, the system automatically knows it's a bug fix and prepares a patch bump.

143
00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:38.920
If they write feat, the system preps a minor bump.

144
00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:42.920
And if they include the exact text breaking change, it triggers a major bump.

145
00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:46.000
By standardizing this, you can generate two things automatically,

146
00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:48.120
a highly technical change log for your developers,

147
00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:52.320
and high-level release notes focusing purely on value for your customers and PMs.

148
00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:56.000
And here is what that seamless flow actually looks like

149
00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.320
when you use an automation tool like Symantec Release.

150
00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:01.360
A developer merges their code into the main branch.

151
00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:04.660
The tool automatically parses those commit prefixes we just talked about.

152
00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:08.860
It calculates the exact next semantic version with literally zero human intervention.

153
00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:13.240
It then creates an immutable git tag, which is a permanent anchor point in your code history,

154
00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:14.780
and publishes the release notes.

155
00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:19.000
A simple feat tag effortlessly becomes a polished, fully documented release.

156
00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:20.420
It's totally foolproof.

157
00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:22.700
So what does this all mean for us?

158
00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:27.140
Whether you're a product manager steering the roadmap or a developer writing the actual commits,

159
00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:28.600
Please remember this.

160
00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:30.180
Versioning is not bureaucracy.

161
00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:32.380
It's a language of predictability.

162
00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:41.280
Avoiding commitment by leaving your software in an endless zero-ver state, you know, never hitting version 1.0, that's an anti-pattern that genuinely hurts your product.

163
00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:47.940
Consistent semantic versioning is hands down the cheapest, most effective way to build and maintain trust with your users.

164
00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:49.400
So I'll leave you with this thought.

165
00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:51.100
Take a look at your latest release.

166
00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:53.680
Are your version numbers actually keeping their promises?

167
00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:55.600
Thanks for learning with me today.

